== Glossary of Administrative Terms

The following terms appear throughout the handbook and in the Expense Portal. Definitions are administrative, not entitlement-bearing: nothing here creates, limits, or amends any reimbursement rule. Where a term is defined differently in a statutory context, the statutory context governs for statutory purposes and this glossary governs for internal filing purposes.

#table(
  columns: (1.1fr, 3.4fr),
  align: (left, left),
  fill: (_, y) => if y == 0 { rgb("#dbeafe") } else if calc.even(y) { rgb("#f8fafc") } else { none },
  stroke: 0.5pt + rgb("#cbd5e1"),
  table.header[*Term*][*Definition*],
  [Folio], [The itemized account issued by a hotel at check-out. A folio lists every posting to the room account and is the only acceptable proof of accommodation spend.],
  [Claim cycle], [The recurring window in which the Expense Auditing Team processes submissions. Cycles close at the end of each working week; a claim filed on Friday evening is processed in the next cycle.],
  [Cost center], [The internal account against which a claim is posted. Every claim carries exactly one cost center, selected at submission; redistribution between cost centers after posting requires Finance sign-off.],
  [Delegate], [A colleague authorized to prepare a claim on another colleague's behalf. Delegation is set in the portal profile and does not transfer liability: the named claimant remains the claimant.],
  [Attestation], [A short written confirmation, recorded in the portal note, that a stated condition was met. Attestations are checked for plausibility like any other documentation.],
  [Receipt lineage], [The chain from original vendor document to the scanned file attached in the portal. A claim with intact lineage can be traced to the counter sale without leaving the portal.],
  [Reconciliation window], [The period between a card transaction posting and its matching claim being marked verified. Items that linger in the window appear on the monthly cardholder statement of open items.],
  [Vendor master record], [The central registry of suppliers with whom the firm holds payment terms. A supplier must exist in the vendor master record before Accounts Payable can remit to it.],
  [Framework agreement], [A negotiated supply arrangement covering a category of common spend. Framework terms apply automatically; claimants cannot opt into worse terms with a better pen.],
  [Fiscal locale], [The jurisdiction whose documentation conventions apply to a claim, determined by where the spend occurred. Determines receipt language and invoice format expectations.],
  [Settlement rate], [The exchange rate applied by the card scheme at the moment a foreign-currency transaction is billed. The settlement rate is recorded by the portal and is not recalculated.],
  [Service queue], [The shared mailbox and ticketing view through which a body receives requests. Queues are worked in arrival order, save for duty-of-care matters, which jump the line.],
  [Wind-down window], [The notice period between the retirement of a supplier or process and its actual cessation. During a wind-down window both the old and new routes are valid.],
  [Duty of care], [The firm's obligation to know where its travelling staff are and to assist them in an emergency. Duty-of-care requirements always outrank filing neatness.],
  [Open item], [A card transaction or claim that has not yet reached a verified state. Open items age visibly in the portal; nobody wants a centenarian open item at audit time.],
  [Post-trip report], [The structured summary attached to certain claims, recording where, when, and for whom the trip occurred. It is descriptive, not argumentative.],
  [Record class], [The archival category assigned to a document, determining its storage location and retention schedule. Record classes are assigned by Records & Facilities Administration.],
  [Business purpose], [The sentence in a claim note explaining what the expenditure was for and whom it served. Every claim has one; most claims have a short one.],
)

== Workflow Checklists: Submission, Approval, and Archiving

The three checklists below restate the firm's process steps in one place. They are process aids, not new rules: every item traces back to a chapter of this handbook, and the chapter governs if the wording here ever drifts from it. Print them, pin them, or simply leave them open in a browser tab during your first month.

=== Submission checklist (claimant)

- [ ] Confirm the spend is of a type this handbook reimburses at all.
- [ ] Collect the original, itemized vendor document; a payment terminal slip alone is not proof.
- [ ] Check the receipt shows the vendor's name, date, and itemization in the expected format.
- [ ] Write the note the relevant chapter requires, stating facts rather than feelings.
- [ ] Deduct personal and non-reimbursable lines from the total before entry where the handbook requires self-deduction.
- [ ] Select the correct cost center and attach the claim to the right client project code where one exists.
- [ ] Verify every attached scan is legible, right side up, and complete, including the reverse of double-sided receipts.
- [ ] Submit within the claim cycle that gives you the calmest quarter-end.

=== Approval checklist (line manager)

- [ ] Confirm the claimant was on the trip, at the meeting, or in the office the claim describes.
- [ ] Confirm the note states what the handbook requires for that receipt type, not merely something.
- [ ] Confirm self-deductions have been made where required, and spot-check one in three claims for arithmetic.
- [ ] Confirm the cost center and project coding match the engagement the spend served.
- [ ] Confirm the claim falls inside the claimant's normal pattern; unusual patterns go to the Expense Auditing Team with a question, not an automatic rejection.
- [ ] Approve or return the claim with a specific written reason; "see policy" is not a reason.

=== Archiving checklist (claimant and approver)

- [ ] Leave the claim in verified status; do not reopen verified claims to "tidy" the wording.
- [ ] Retain originals of any document marked as original-only by Records & Facilities Administration, and hand them to the office administrator for physical archiving.
- [ ] Confirm project-related receipts are also filed in the engagement workspace where the client contract requires it.
- [ ] On departure from the firm, hand over all open items and original documents to your delegate or line manager before the final working day.
- [ ] Respond to any audit query on an archived claim through the Expense Auditing Team channel, quoting the claim reference; never send originals to third parties without Records & Facilities Administration involvement.

A claim that has passed through all three checklists needs nothing further from anyone. That, ultimately, is the entire administrative philosophy of this appendix.
